# Gemstone Caverns' luck counter changes only its own printed "{T}: Add {C}" (CR 605.1a): the "instead" is part of
# that one ability's text. A mana ability the land has been granted keeps adding what it says (CR 613.1f), so after
# Rain of Filth the Caverns' "Sacrifice this land: Add {B}" still adds black, not a color of the player's choice.
#
# Bot2 is not the starting player, so the Caverns begins the game on the battlefield with a luck counter (the two
# lines before any turn's). On its first turn Bot2 plays a Swamp for Rain of Filth, taps the lucky Caverns for red,
# then sacrifices it for black. The sacrifice line names no color: were the grant rewritten to "any color", the
# prompt would ask one and the default answer would not be black.
#
# Rain of Filth always takes the later layer-6 timestamp here (the Caverns has been out since before turn one), so
# this is the order an every-ability rewrite happened to get right; PrintedManaAbilityRewriteGrantTests puts a
# test-local granter first, the order that one got wrong.

[Bot1 Deck]
Forest
Forest
Forest
Forest
Forest
Forest
Forest
Forest x10

[Bot2 Deck]
Gemstone Caverns
Rain of Filth
Swamp
Plains
Plains
Plains
Plains
Plains x10

[Actions]
Bot2.Accept
Bot2.Choose(Plains)
Bot2.Hand.Swamp.Play
Bot2.Battlefield.Swamp.Activate
Bot2.Hand.Rain of Filth.Cast
Bot2.Pass
Bot1.Pass
# The printed ability, still lucky: one mana of any color.
Bot2.Battlefield.Gemstone Caverns.Activate(Ability: {T}; Color: Red)
# The granted ability, untouched by the luck counter: black.
Bot2.Battlefield.Gemstone Caverns.Activate(Ability: Sacrifice)
[Handover Bot2.Pass]

[Expect]
Bot2.Graveyard = Gemstone Caverns; Rain of Filth
Bot2.Mana = {B}{R}
Exile = Plains
